#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "6010c0f3-67d3-5b21-af26-695a864fb42a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3541" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\sin{x}-2\\cos{x}$ can be written as $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $0^\\circ < \\phi < 90^\\circ$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $\\phi$?" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 2; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{2} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= 1 \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (1) \\\\\n&= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "91e6008e-5c37-55aa-8cf6-cac61198f459" :question/math-academy-id "e-3542" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x$ can be written as $R\\sin(x+\\phi)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R$?" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x + ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ + \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x + R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = R\\sin(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\sin x + \\sqrt{7}\\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x + (R \\sin \\phi) \\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = \\sqrt{2}; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= \\sqrt{2}^{2} + \\sqrt{7}^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 2 + 7 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 9 \\\\\nR &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "557b1b6e-c54a-5fa9-85e1-3cea346c3bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3543" :question/problem "Write $3\\sin x-2\\cos x$ in the form $R\\sin(x-\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$. Round $\\phi$ to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\sin (x - ϕ) &= R(\\sin x\\cos ϕ - \\cos x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\sin x - R\\sin ϕ\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = R\\sin(x-\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3 \\sin x - 2 \\cos x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\sin x - (R \\sin \\phi)\\cos x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = 2\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.588rad\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\cos ϕ)^{2} + (R\\sin ϕ)^{2} &= (3)^{2} + (2)^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\cos^{2} ϕ + \\sin^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 4 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 13 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only considered the positive root, since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\sin(x)-2\\cos(x) \\approx \\sqrt{13} \\sin \\left(x - 0.588 \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "9ea36397-ab9d-5567-8b38-3e83ba624e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3544" :question/problem "Write $3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x$ in the form $R\\cos(x+\\phi)$ with $\\phi \\in \\left(0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR\\cos (x + ϕ) &= R(\\cos x\\cos ϕ - \\sin x\\sin ϕ) \\\\\n&= R\\cos ϕ\\cos x - R\\sin ϕ\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = R\\cos(x+\\phi)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = (R \\cos \\phi) \\cos x -(R \\sin \\phi)\\sin x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{R\\cos ϕ = 3; R\\sin ϕ = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{R\\sin ϕ}{R\\cos ϕ} &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\tan ϕ &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\nϕ &= \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R$. Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(R\\sin ϕ)^{2} + (R\\cos ϕ)^{2} &= 3^{2} + (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\\nR^{2}(\\sin^{2} ϕ + \\cos^{2} ϕ) &= 9 + 3 \\\\\nR^{2} &= 12 \\\\\nR &= \\sqrt{12} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0$.\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\cos x - \\sqrt{3}\\sin x = 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2fbe32d-2471-5f31-8937-5a976aacbe57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266545} {:answer/id #uuid "574fdd06-62ef-5e9c-8a30-c14e34e944a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266546} {:answer/id #uuid "c4329340-cdd7-5d78-adec-08a44ee33696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266547} {:answer/id #uuid "876e88e1-61e8-5548-b36d-b7350aca0bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266548} {:answer/id #uuid "deb73b4d-868c-5b65-8b24-03f60af02e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "876e88e1-61e8-5548-b36d-b7350aca0bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266548} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc53fca3-abf7-574e-8d39-b66758efc0b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266544}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceb99c5e-97cb-59e6-8416-51c12a07092e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107553" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =1+3 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =4 \\\\ R & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00295e9c-f622-5e51-a7ee-b2097ff85a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266552} {:answer/id #uuid "5c747491-7444-568e-ac19-2fecc1eb9bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266553} {:answer/id #uuid "2b788dc0-c61a-5e5b-8d1b-69349143796c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266554} {:answer/id #uuid "839cdaa3-d51b-52d0-bb31-9992acb6d8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266555} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffbd437-b183-5a89-ba17-d9b394cf8c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00295e9c-f622-5e51-a7ee-b2097ff85a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266552} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5c9a875-3fdd-5ea4-9aca-2861eb449a9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266551}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6325006-eee7-5703-9b1d-10b07ccf030a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107558" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =3+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =12 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21967a74-a70c-5d80-9061-e2f8238e3b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266559} {:answer/id #uuid "ce34e396-33a4-584c-9f1d-8f7056a38a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266560} {:answer/id #uuid "623ea025-1e92-59f8-926d-0d25222e7e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266561} {:answer/id #uuid "ec117651-6ebe-569e-b39f-3266d56d5381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a963b7b-2737-594d-9e91-6c643c1b46ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce34e396-33a4-584c-9f1d-8f7056a38a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266560} :answer-field/id #uuid "c858464a-8f09-513f-bda6-85d1356abf8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266558}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3afc8de-0496-5d66-b501-f8daf7600278" :question/math-academy-id "q-107565" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ca2e08-c2e0-511e-bda0-599a38f224b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266566} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7ba58a-798a-5807-b249-708a5c5b9a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266567} {:answer/id #uuid "e1827cd8-b4b5-5ddf-9256-bc067ce409d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266568} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf03b85-b86e-5971-811c-8e42af13f8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266569} {:answer/id #uuid "71055321-8af4-5fbd-8e12-a326c82c546d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf03b85-b86e-5971-811c-8e42af13f8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266569} :answer-field/id #uuid "a45f2153-4683-5fa9-9617-143881db98aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266565}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29f6ea73-9de7-55dd-ae3c-4a1047548a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-107570" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =10 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "959266a1-b43b-5ccf-85f5-33feb2ee6293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266573} {:answer/id #uuid "ca59d549-212d-5afa-a3a0-be266414dd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266574} {:answer/id #uuid "3415ec0b-63df-5359-ada7-30638d1b4f16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+0.464)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266575} {:answer/id #uuid "10598812-0f6a-5d50-bf5a-847740aef2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.351)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266576} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f504f-5d44-5838-a29e-902c6f03b066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.150)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca59d549-212d-5afa-a3a0-be266414dd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266574} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8723bed-db8d-58c9-bc0f-1d4124803bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db0aa66b-7e8e-5027-b840-3d5fc4d9828f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107577" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =2 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\  & =1.107\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(2\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =5+4\\cdot 5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+1.107)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0147be66-1ffa-5d03-8fc2-549e0a14ec7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266580} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb20a8-f88e-5089-a8b6-fa06637d95e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266581} {:answer/id #uuid "d33c017c-0256-5ca6-9698-b9cd6cc16487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266582} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7ca137-6015-53f7-b7a1-37ec317613a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266583} {:answer/id #uuid "25236670-2fc6-5770-9bd5-2a0945d575e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25236670-2fc6-5770-9bd5-2a0945d575e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266584} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d3fbc20-6bd4-55e8-837d-1abcc6494356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266579}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b668062-1203-5c59-8b5f-fabcb7916e31" :question/math-academy-id "q-107579" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcd55a3b-d2f6-532f-a8a7-9e3d87cd177f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.983)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266587} {:answer/id #uuid "54295dc2-6ea1-517e-a767-26ef7d899b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.415)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266588} {:answer/id #uuid "58bc3898-4677-5ce6-8dc4-1dbe681cde2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.983)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266589} {:answer/id #uuid "31db00b1-6e93-58eb-89e3-516c7fa18a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.341)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266590} {:answer/id #uuid "3bffc759-a98c-5e1c-afb4-124935b6cb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.983)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcd55a3b-d2f6-532f-a8a7-9e3d87cd177f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.983)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266587} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9ba608-5fa8-506a-9a4b-ae53a1df3160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266586}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc540d3e-160b-508c-ac71-1f1bf7873440" :question/math-academy-id "q-11290" :question/problem "$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ ϕ & ≈0.983\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ) & ={3}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ)) & =9+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =13 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+0.983)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f953393-db97-5831-b323-7470d8f8b027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f953393-db97-5831-b323-7470d8f8b027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266594} :answer-field/id #uuid "2910800d-3403-5c44-ab6a-1d19da820ae7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266593}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d578acc-7046-58d2-ae73-9435d8a6fb2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-274585" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the **exact** value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ \n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7b17ec-8aaa-5772-a0fc-5d2c5bee28d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b7b17ec-8aaa-5772-a0fc-5d2c5bee28d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266597} :answer-field/id #uuid "04c55e61-c577-55c9-90b3-2c61887c4e82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266596}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cec72bc3-9ce9-56a6-be97-f8888cf3eb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-274609" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e2dfc68-3038-51cc-ae0b-0fd760e9168e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266600} {:answer/id #uuid "0657045b-0b3e-56fc-8062-ef4ab99cbe25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0657045b-0b3e-56fc-8062-ef4ab99cbe25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266601} :answer-field/id #uuid "1148039f-d63d-5289-8426-0bf26f7e02d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266599}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f4bdd81-7209-5e16-bfc5-a1fd0121d6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-274614" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0,$ what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055b7f47-2047-59ac-ab6b-db10bd1d99bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266604} {:answer/id #uuid "1b05f1ba-cbdb-5c22-ae61-26fd8698cfd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266605} {:answer/id #uuid "7d80862d-ceed-5218-a10a-5fa7310fcca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266606} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae4900d-1807-5425-9844-93cf3e86d8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266607} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9b90bb-0591-5edb-8707-f322beaccd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b05f1ba-cbdb-5c22-ae61-26fd8698cfd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266605} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fa1131c-da92-5cd8-8b0a-6ac1d605eea1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266603}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fd10193-69cb-5212-ac5c-9e4d5831b0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-30588" :question/problem "$\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =3 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74765624-2997-556c-bb1d-96da05bb7da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266611} {:answer/id #uuid "b22eb970-03fb-5ee4-b8e1-f0ace298ff5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74765624-2997-556c-bb1d-96da05bb7da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266611} :answer-field/id #uuid "1003564f-4545-5b97-90dc-4d5adaced41e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266610}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50f75dc0-69cb-5287-84d4-979b01969621" :question/math-academy-id "q-327669" :question/problem "Given that the expression $7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=7 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={7}^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =49+72 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =121 \\\\ R & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf88c5a6-9ff2-57e0-a58b-d8095138c533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf88c5a6-9ff2-57e0-a58b-d8095138c533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266615} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed1c6879-8d2a-52e2-a9f8-f6b0da0ebbeb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266614}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1804472b-fa65-568f-bf51-ed15b870bb4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327678" :question/problem "Given that the expression $9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=9 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={9}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =81+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =90 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{90} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3dba0779-e903-5281-bc41-01b78c31a98c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "585412c9-2d85-50eb-a64f-b1ad71c726b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266618} {:answer/id #uuid "11d8922f-cbac-584f-969f-c0bc1f9816b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11d8922f-cbac-584f-969f-c0bc1f9816b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266619} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd3a8a0-03b2-5b72-847f-09066e84d130" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266617}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ccdd617-bac1-5cc2-b91d-e1cdd0bac4ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-327703" :question/problem "Given that the expression $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =18 \\\\ R & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d51fa9-e55e-56a6-b3c7-3bfdcb6468f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266622} {:answer/id #uuid "34cd456b-03d7-5b40-b2d4-4bf35537c125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d51fa9-e55e-56a6-b3c7-3bfdcb6468f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266622} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4a84f0-1887-5f8a-8f60-ebafa1362b37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266621}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4162ecbe-b322-51b0-80d6-aa2e8ed16fcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-327979" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0,$ what is the **exact** value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($ \n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f8339f-00ac-51ff-996f-b705e179817c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266626} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a13f3f-a856-5d39-9b1a-1ed8a5151b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266627} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e5e227-e80a-5231-a90b-1120051bba7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266628} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b78dd8-b7d2-51da-9446-b85be5763818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266629} {:answer/id #uuid "f13246d2-9194-57db-bffb-eda892501972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47f8339f-00ac-51ff-996f-b705e179817c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266626} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d04b26-e8e6-5c47-8533-c35a36b5a75e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266625}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "87b7b279-7595-56ef-b4fc-02a3f4fc0572" :question/math-academy-id "q-65583" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-ϕ)$ with $0<ϕ<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=8 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{8} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b464c4c-5424-5b68-90ee-8a3a1ea2aac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266633} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf5d6ae-541c-58fc-9f0a-7735c442be3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{25}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266634} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce88fb7-b45e-5f46-81b8-ad8bc470c0b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266635} {:answer/id #uuid "a31f2a35-6f7b-5611-8f23-1befe5b4efac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266636} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bd6588-8fe7-558c-9582-625908683063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31f2a35-6f7b-5611-8f23-1befe5b4efac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266636} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c156bbb-7dea-54d4-a160-8c5346495408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266632}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "173b9c9b-2704-59ce-883e-5a93fff13ecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80134" :question/problem "Given that the expression $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ ϕ & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd174f26-5fb1-5c28-891e-44b1e55f59dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266640} {:answer/id #uuid "c078a503-fa99-5853-a262-b1e85aa6104b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266641} {:answer/id #uuid "88a1db87-659b-529e-bc5c-74574970ddc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266642} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ce1e94-2fa2-5964-96e0-99175e831e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266643} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a9f6a9-8147-5b11-b7bf-290651e0f5cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88a1db87-659b-529e-bc5c-74574970ddc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266642} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8ca054-fb1e-5181-962f-3eb7e82fa997" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266639}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a550d3a-2b5d-57a0-a5f6-6861c54f511f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80136" :question/problem "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{8}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =4 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df969370-b77a-5be5-a0d2-e799792bf0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266647} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2f0117-d544-539b-b124-c48f8d1c1dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266648} {:answer/id #uuid "9003f9cc-5c96-5514-a21b-f2d188f5ea67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266649} {:answer/id #uuid "6901f3e0-5624-5f77-a3aa-87c4984f6297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266650} {:answer/id #uuid "408a0019-85f9-54c0-ad93-61d915471af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2f0117-d544-539b-b124-c48f8d1c1dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266648} :answer-field/id #uuid "744e7dfb-4457-5ac4-a821-f53b644c029b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266646}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7349c862-48b2-5cc6-aa44-b59bdf12d6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80138" :question/problem "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={4}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =32 \\\\ R & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a962bea-050d-5472-8938-5bbdea366289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.101)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266654} {:answer/id #uuid "c975570d-e26c-50d5-b151-96a7f4be162c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.120)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266655} {:answer/id #uuid "69729c8c-6055-570d-b3d4-e6bb127d198a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.710)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266656} {:answer/id #uuid "aa166b74-294f-5416-a234-63522777fe9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.035)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266657} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff6401a-80a6-550a-ae1b-f5073686ff42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266658}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff6401a-80a6-550a-ae1b-f5073686ff42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266658} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9e3e3e-1ec7-5b80-a593-77f23ba1d408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266653}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84950e34-628d-580f-b5fe-6a932ca0b240" :question/math-academy-id "q-80144" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =2 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2) \\\\ ϕ & ≈1.107\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{R}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ) & ={2}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡(ϕ)+{cos}^{2}⁡(ϕ)) & =4+1 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =5 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)≈\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1.107)\n$$"}]}
